1051	{"artifactId":"LLD-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-s4","entry":{"id":"7aeb3a3e","text":"Revised 2026-10-11 after the first daemon review (block: 2 HIGH, 3 MED) and the stakeholder's decision to run the step tools inside the daemon (Story s11). (1) The analyze step runs in the MCP server's process and reads the live store directly, far beyond its cache; the cache move is taken out of this Story and the whole move belongs to s11. Until s11 lands the MCP server is let in by a temporary permitted-reader declaration, which s11 deletes; acceptance criterion ac1 is met in full only then. (2) The status handler guards its store read so that it answers after a store failure. (3) Reads have no wrapper; getGraphStore raises the failure. (4) A stop of a stopped daemon stays an error, as today. (5) The control script's start shares the forced-stop function with its stop. The pid check reuses DAEMON_ENTRY.","actor":{"id":"legacy:subho-ghosh","kind":"person","properties":{"name":"Subho Ghosh"}},"timestamp":"2026-10-11T08:36:26.977Z","feedback":[],"kind":"comment"}}
1093	{"artifactId":"LLD-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-s4","entry":{"id":"a0bc4efc","text":"Revised twice more on 2026-10-11, after the second and third daemon reviews (blocks of 5 and 4). Detection now follows a measurement of the failed store: a read throws -30775, a synchronous transaction throws 22, an asynchronous transaction never settles; so the asynchronous wrapper bounds its wait and probes with a read, the synchronous wrapper confirms code 22 with the same probe, the request server and queue catch a failing read, and the periodic reader check probes too. Also: the CLI review reaches source roots through a daemon request; stopDaemon bounds its request and never removes the pid file of a live non-matching process; the refusal of the store files is called from the copy and move tools themselves; reportFatal and a stop with the store failed end by self-kill without waiting for the queue. The third round's fixes are NOT re-reviewed.","actor":{"id":"legacy:subho-ghosh","kind":"person","properties":{"name":"Subho Ghosh"}},"timestamp":"2026-10-11T08:47:09.017Z","feedback":[],"kind":"comment"}}
1157	{"artifactId":"LLD-c2ba6fa62a148ed1-s4","entry":{"id":"1d73c638","text":"Correction found while building Task t3 on 2026-10-11 (src/db/graph/__tests__/lock-loss-reproduction.test.ts). This design says a read on a store whose locks were removed throws code -30775. That holds only for a process that has never read from the store. A process that has read before, which the daemon always has because the store module reads while opening, fails a read with code 22 and a message containing MDB_BAD_RSLOT (\"Invalid reuse of reader locktable slot: The reader lock pid 0 ... does not match env pid\"). The earlier measurement used a process that had not read. Consequence for Tasks t4 and t5: wherever this design says a read or probe \"throws -30775\", the rule is \"throws -30775, or throws code 22 with MDB_BAD_RSLOT in its message\". A bare code 22 from a synchronous transaction is still only confirmed by the probe read. The asynchronous and synchronous write behaviours are as designed.","actor":{"id":"legacy:claude-builder","kind":"person","properties":{"name":"Claude (builder)"}},"timestamp":"2026-10-11T09:58:16.728Z","feedback":[],"kind":"comment"}}
